<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tsongkha</h1><p class="st-meta">1008 CE – 1104 CE (approx.) · capital Qingtang (Xining)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Tibetan kingdom in Amdo, centred on modern Xining, that allied with the Song against the Tanguts and controlled a southern branch of the Silk Road.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire</a> fragmented, Tibetan tribes in the Huangshui valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amdo">Amdo</a> brought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusiluo">Gusiluo</a>, a youth claiming descent from the old royal line, around 1008. After years as a figurehead of local strongmen he established his own court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xining">Qingtang</a> (modern Xining) around 1032.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusiluo">Gusiluo</a> was a Buddhist ruler presiding over a loose federation of Tibetan clans. He allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, which gave him titles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>, and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanguts">Tangut</a> attacks from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-xia">Western Xia</a> in the 1030s. When Tangut wars disrupted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a>, caravans used the Qinghai route through his territory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xining">Qingtang</a> grew rich on trade in horses and other goods.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusiluo">Gusiluo's</a> death in 1065 the kingdom was divided among his descendants and weakened by feuds. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> annexed the region in campaigns of 1099 and 1104, though Song control was soon lost to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty</a> (1115-1234) and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-xia">Western Xia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tsongkha.jpg" alt="Tsongkha" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Qiushufang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Era_of_Fragmentation.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1008 CE – 1104 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qingtang (Xining)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gusiluo, Dongzhan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gusiluo regime, Qingtang, Tsongkha Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1056&amp;focus=tsongkha" aria-label="Tsongkha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7445 1006 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tsongkha lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7765 1262h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7765 1262h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=1056&amp;focus=tsongkha">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

